  "summary": "Shining Force CD is Sonic! Software Planning's tactical role-playing game, first released for Mega-CD in Japan on 22 July 1994.",
  "body": [
    "Shining Force CD is Sonic! Software Planning's tactical role-playing game, first released for Mega-CD in Japan on 22 July 1994. Its first two books adapt Shining Force Gaiden and Gaiden II from Game Gear, followed by two new scenarios that continue their stories. The player leads a force through turn-based battles, choosing where individual characters move and which opponent or ally receives their next action. Short scenes connect the encounters, giving the campaign a concentrated rhythm of preparation, a battlefield problem and the consequences that lead into the next book.",
    "The fighting follows the Gaiden games' tactical structure, with individual units moving across a map and acting when their turn arrives. Between battles, the player can save, promote characters, revive fallen companions and sometimes buy or sell equipment. These pauses keep the force ready without requiring a separate free-roaming town. A unit's development matters across successive encounters, so choosing how to use a weaker character also concerns the army's future. The next map draws on that preparation, asking the player to bring the available strengths together around another arrangement of enemies and terrain.",
    "Completing the first two books opens the third, while the fourth also requires a hidden item. Character statistics can carry into those later scenarios through a backup RAM cartridge, extending the value of the force already developed. The hardware's internal memory is insufficient to preserve all the necessary book data together, making the accessory relevant to that continuity. Motoaki Takenouchi composed the score. Shining Force CD uses the disc as a home for a linked campaign, expanding two portable adventures into a longer sequence whose later challenges depend on the party built earlier."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Four books of tactical battles",
    "The force between encounters",
    "Carrying a party into another chapter"
  ],
